  <div class="xuanji-main">
    <div class="xuanji-gridwrap">
      <% cell_size = 22 %>
      <% pad = 10 %>
      <% w = pad*2 + cell_size*@grid.width %>
      <% h = pad*2 + cell_size*@grid.height %>

      <svg
        class="xuanji-svg"
        viewBox="0 0 <%= w %> <%= h %>"
        width="<%= w %>"
        height="<%= h %>"
        aria-label="<%= t("fun.xuanji.grid_aria") %>"
        data-action="pointerdown->xuanji#pointerDown pointermove->xuanji#pointerMove pointerup->xuanji#pointerUp pointercancel->xuanji#pointerUp"
      >
        <%#
          第三圖, drawn as lines.

          Kang says to do it this way and says why: 第三圖 is the lattice on
          rows and columns 1, 8, 15, 22, 29 together with both diagonals, and
          it crosses every other figure — row 15 runs straight through the
          middle of the central square. Colouring those cells red would make
          第三圖 fight with 第五圖, 第六圖 and 第七圖 for the same characters:

            但餘圖有於此圖借字有礙　今推縱横初行八行紅書
            余以紅線界畫即準之　讀者審焉

          So the red CHARACTERS are rows and columns 1, 8, 22 and 29, and
          第三圖 is ruled over the top in red line. That is also what makes the
          five colours tile the square exactly — the lattice is not competing
          for cells, it is drawn on them.

          29 = 1 + 4x7, which is why the lines fall where they do and why they
          cut the square into bands of 6, 13 and 6 each way.
        %>
        <% centre = ->(i) { pad + (i - 1) * cell_size + cell_size / 2.0 } %>
        <% first = centre.(1) %>
        <% last = centre.(@grid.width) %>

        <g class="xuanji-rulings" aria-hidden="true">
          <% [1, 8, 15, 22, 29].each do |i| %>
            <line class="xuanji-ruling" x1="<%= first %>" y1="<%= centre.(i) %>"
                  x2="<%= last %>" y2="<%= centre.(i) %>" />
            <line class="xuanji-ruling" x1="<%= centre.(i) %>" y1="<%= first %>"
                  x2="<%= centre.(i) %>" y2="<%= last %>" />
          <% end %>
          <%# 仁嗟斜至春親 and 琴廊斜至基津 — the two diagonals of the 采式. %>
          <line class="xuanji-ruling xuanji-ruling-diagonal"
                x1="<%= first %>" y1="<%= first %>" x2="<%= last %>" y2="<%= last %>" />
          <line class="xuanji-ruling xuanji-ruling-diagonal"
                x1="<%= first %>" y1="<%= last %>" x2="<%= last %>" y2="<%= first %>" />
        </g>
        <% indexed = @grid.xuanji_cells.index_by { |c| [c.x, c.y] } %>
        <% @grid.height.times do |y| %>
          <% @grid.width.times do |x| %>
            <% c = indexed.fetch([x,y]) %>
            <%#
              康萬民's five: 朱墨青紫黄. The enum carries six because the
              Wikisource 「fontcolor」 markup used six hex values, and the two
              blues are both 青 — which runs from blue through turquoise, so
              navy and teal are one colour here and always have been.

              The regions those five cover tile the square exactly:
              朱 216, 墨 144, 青 312, 紫 80, 黄 89, which is 841. See
              Xuanji::PaintFigures, whose `audit` checks a grid against them.
            %>
            <% canonical_color =
              case c.color
              when "red", "maroon" then "red"
              when "black" then "black"
              when "blue", "navy", "teal" then "blue"
              when "yellow", "olive" then "yellow"
              when "purple" then "purple"
              else nil
              end
            %>
            <% fill = canonical_color ? "var(--xuanji-#{canonical_color})" : "#333333" %>
            <% rx = pad + x*cell_size %>
            <% ry = pad + y*cell_size %>

            <g class="xuanji-cell" data-x="<%= x %>" data-y="<%= y %>">
              <rect class="xuanji-rect" id="xuanji-rect-<%= x %>-<%= y %>" x="<%= rx %>" y="<%= ry %>" width="<%= cell_size %>" height="<%= cell_size %>" />
              <text class="xuanji-text" id="xuanji-text-<%= x %>-<%= y %>" x="<%= rx + cell_size*0.5 %>" y="<%= ry + cell_size*0.75 %>" text-anchor="middle" fill="<%= fill %>"><%= c.char %></text>
            </g>
          <% end %>
        <% end %>

      </svg>
    </div>

    <div class="xuanji-output">
      <h2><%= t("fun.xuanji.extracted_text") %></h2>

      <%#
        Whether the reading rhymes, and by whose rhyme book.

        康萬民 explains his own count in exactly these terms:

          余増讀詩内多叶古韻　起宗所遺或於韻學有未盡諳乎此　余用古韻而讀益多也

        So rhyme is not a gloss on this figure — it is the test that decides
        whether a path through the grid is a poem. The panel shows the finals of
        the even-numbered lines, which is where a quatrain rhymes, and then one
        row per rhyme book: 叶 where they agree, · where they do not, ? where
        the book does not list enough of them to say.

        Several books, because no single one accounts for all his poems. 讀法四's
        經情清明英榮 rhymes under 洪武正韻 and under 攝 and not under 王力's
        先秦韻部; 讀法五's 妃闈飛衣誰追 is the other way about. Over 592 generated
        paths, 洪武正韻 admits 178 and 王力 163, and the two together admit 219 —
        so 古韻 is not looser than 今韻 but differently cut, and the count grows
        because holding both books admits poems neither admits alone. Which is
        what Kang says happened to 起宗.
      %>
      <div class="xuanji-rhyme" data-xuanji-target="rhyme" aria-live="polite"></div>

      <div class="corpus-reader" data-controller="corpus-reader" data-corpus-reader-target="viewbox">
        <pre data-xuanji-target="output" data-corpus-reader-target="content"></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
